#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf65e50-bb88-5c21-8317-13393f4c132b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3,\\,v=\\frac{3}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342209} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f9421d-8284-5c82-bcd4-0dacb6cfc98d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342210} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bb9091-9ef3-5320-86d5-25b0773bc71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342211} {:answer/id #uuid "e06fa6db-c8e6-5a00-9d6c-6be9ec3bed60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342212} {:answer/id #uuid "8a288eab-e1f2-5e59-9902-802f5d8787ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3bb9091-9ef3-5320-86d5-25b0773bc71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342211} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0710a0c-afe0-53b4-85ec-60d8d04fc9a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9016b9c4-555f-5f54-8e92-6ab14881289d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73243" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t{e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((1-t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-2){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=2.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(1-(2)){e}^{-(2)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
